​1. System Requirements & Setup
​Prerequisites - ​Before running the simulator, ensure your system has the following software installed:
- ​Python 3.8 or higher
- ​Flask (pip install flask)
- ​Any modern web browser (Google Chrome, Mozilla Firefox, Microsoft Edge, or Safari)

​Installation & Launch Instructions
1. Directory Structure - Ensure your project folder contains the following structure:
project-root/
│
├── app.py                   # Flask server wrapper
├── algorithm.py             # Core SRTF simulation backend
├── samplejobs.csv           # Preset job manifest dataset
└── interface/               # Frontend asset directory
    ├── index.html           # Structure and controls
    ├── style.css            # Custom CSS layout & theme
    └── script.js            # Animation ticker & UI controller

2. Start the Flask Server - Open a terminal or command prompt in your project root folder and execute:
python app.py

3. Access the Interface - Open your web browser and navigate to: 
http://127.0.0.1:5000/


2. Interface Overview
​The user interface is organized into six functional sections:
​Header - Displays the project title (Shortest Remaining Time First Simulator) and course description (CSP108 – Operating System Midterm Project).
​Job Input Section - Contains the input fields, dataset action buttons, and the Job Input Table displaying all registered processes.
​Controls Deck - Features execution controls (Start, Pause, Reset), an animation Speed selector, and a real-time Simulation Clock Ticker.
​Live Simulation Area:
- ​CPU Box - Shows the currently executing job ID and its dynamic remaining burst time.
- ​Ready Queue Container - Displays color-coded badges for all processes currently waiting in line.
​Event Log Section - Displays a scrolling, time-stamped text record of every process event (arrivals, dispatches, preemptions, and completions).
​Gantt Chart & Results Section - Renders a color-coded visual execution timeline alongside a summary table with comprehensive performance statistics.

​3. Step-by-Step Operating Guide
​Step 1: Loading or Entering Process Data
​You can populate jobs into the scheduling table using two methods:

​Option A: Loading Sample CSV Dataset
- ​Click the "Load Sample Jobs (CSV)" button.
- ​The system fetches samplejobs.csv via the backend API and populates the table automatically (e.g., J001: Climate Simulation, J002: Statistical Analysis, etc.).
​Option B: Manual Job Entry
​In the Job Input form, enter the required fields:
- ​Job ID: A unique text identifier (e.g., J008).
- ​Job Name: A descriptive process name (e.g., Database Query).
- ​Arrival Time: A non-negative whole number (e.g., 2).
- ​Estimated Duration: A positive whole number representing the CPU burst length (e.g., 8).
​Click "Add Job".
​Note: To reset the input list, click "Clear Jobs".
